course: dictionary
lesson: 572
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which meaning the writer means. Read the words around the hard word first, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: «"Austerity measures" means cuts in public spending.» The clues are cuts and spending, which are about money. Here, austerity means that a government spends less money, so there are fewer services for people.
explain: Austerity can also mean a plain, simple way of living with no comforts or luxury, like a bare room with only a bed and a chair. A third meaning is strictness or seriousness in how someone looks or acts. Check which meaning fits the sentence.
fact: Austerity is a noun, and its plural is austerities.
fact: «Austerity» means a time when a government cuts spending sharply, so people have less money and fewer services.
fact: «Austerity» can mean a plain, simple way of living without comforts or luxury.
fact: «Austerity» can mean strictness or seriousness in how someone looks or acts.
fact: Useful phrases are austerity measures, a time of austerity, and live in austerity.
exercise: «"Austerity measures" means cuts in public spending.» What does austerity mean in this phrase? | Building lots of new swimming pools | A very long holiday | Giving away free sweets to everyone | *Cutting government spending
exercise: A family had to live in austerity after the war. What does live in austerity mean? | Live in a big house with a swimming pool | Live near the sea and go swimming every day | Live very loudly and happily | *Live with few comforts and no luxuries
exercise: «The headteacher spoke with austerity, and nobody dared to giggle.» What does austerity suggest here? | *Strict and serious | Cheerful and silly | Very noisy and excited | Covered in dust
test: «The monk lived a life of austerity, with one plain bed and one wooden bowl.» What does austerity mean here? | A life of sport and games | *A plain life with no luxuries | A life spent in a noisy city | A life full of parties and treats
test: Which phrase uses austerity to mean cuts in public money? | a sunny austerity | an austerity birthday party | *austerity measures | austerity holiday
exam: Jayden says austerity always means cuts to money. Is he right? | No, it means a very loud sound | Yes, it always means cuts to money | Yes, and it also means a big party | *No, it can also mean a plain life or a stern manner
